You are now in the home stretch. This is the final Creational pattern.
While Factory and Builder are about how to construct an object from scratch, Prototype is about what to do when building
from scratch is simply too expensive.

The Problem: The "Expensive Creation" Bottleneck
Imagine you are building a Strategy Game (like Age of Empires or Starcraft). You need to spawn 1,000 "Orc Grunt"
enemies on the screen.

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer writes a loop that calls new Orc() 1,000 times.
Inside the Orc constructor, the code has to:

    1. Load the 3D mesh from the hard drive.
    2. Load the AI behavior tree from a database.
    3. Calculate base stats based on the current game difficulty.

If this takes 100 milliseconds per Orc, spawning 1,000 Orcs will freeze the game for 1.5 minutes.

Why a Senior rejects this: You shouldn't do the heavy lifting of reading files and databases 1,000 times if the data
is exactly the same!

The Senior Solution: The Prototype Pattern (Cloning)
The Prototype Pattern says: "Build the heavy object exactly ONCE. Then, just use a photocopier to stamp out as many
clones as you need instantly."

Instead of using the new keyword and running the constructor, we give the object a clone() method. The object looks at
its own internal memory and creates an exact duplicate of itself in a fraction of a millisecond.